+.. SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0
+
+============================
+Reserved user virtual ranges
+============================
+
+This downstream ARM64 feature reserves up to eight global virtual address
+intervals for native 64-bit processes. It reserves neither physical memory
+nor placeholder VMAs. Each process allocates mappings independently.
+
+Enable CONFIG_RESERVED_USER_VA. Built-in code can call::
+
+    int reserved_va_register(unsigned int id, unsigned long start,
+                             unsigned long end);
+
+Include <linux/reserved_va.h>. Numbers are 0..7, intervals are [start, end),
+page-aligned, nonempty and within the running machine's user address space.
+Registration must finish synchronously by fs_initcall; fs_initcall_sync
+freezes the registry, before rootfs_initcall enables usermode helpers.
+There is no unregister interface or loadable-module registration export.
+Errors are EINVAL for invalid input, EEXIST for duplicate/overlapping ranges,
+and EPERM after freezing. The registry becomes read-only after init.
+
+CONFIG_OF_RESERVED_USER_VA enables the built-in device tree driver. See
+``Documentation/devicetree/bindings/arm/openeuler,reserved-user-va.yaml``.
+Each enabled child provides range-id and virtual-range (64-bit start, size).
+Malformed/conflicting children are logged and skipped, without undoing
+successful registrations. DTS entries never override built-in registrations.
+
+Userspace
+=========
+
+Use ``MAP_RESERVED_VA_ID(id)`` from the ARM64 UAPI asm/mman.h in mmap flags,
+together with the normal mapping type. For example::
+
+    mmap(NULL, length, PROT_READ | PROT_WRITE,
+         MAP_PRIVATE | MAP_ANONYMOUS | MAP_RESERVED_VA_ID(0), -1, 0);
+
+The enable bit is 0x80; bits 22..24 encode the number. All eight values,
+including zero, require the enable bit. Do not OR multiple numbers together.
+An unregistered number fails with ENODEV. Exhaustion fails with ENOMEM and
+never falls back to another range. A nonfixed address hint outside the
+selected range is ignored. Fixed mappings must fit the selected range in
+full; an ordinary fixed mapping cannot intersect any reserved range.
+Invalid bare number bits and range-policy violations fail with EINVAL.
+When the feature is disabled, reserved flags fail with EOPNOTSUPP.
+
+The facility applies to anonymous, file and device mappings. Existing
+permission, security, offset, huge-page and device alignment requirements
+remain in force. File callbacks retain their original flags and alignment
+decisions. The common empty-area search is constrained using a scoped
+task-local context. A special device allocator that bypasses this search
+must return an address inside the selected range; otherwise the request is
+retried without a hint once, then rejected rather than escaping the range.
+
+VMA splitting, mprotect and fork retain the number; merging across numbers
+is prohibited. mremap retains it for in-place growth and movement, including
+MREMAP_DONTUNMAP. Range-policy rejection of a fixed mremap occurs before
+unmapping the target. munmap releases mappings but does not release the
+global address reservation. Ordinary brk and stack growth cannot enter it.
+
+Configuration
+=============
+
+Choose ranges compatible with the supported executable, stack, heap and
+vDSO layouts. Fixed executable addresses are not relocated to accommodate
+an invalid layout; exec may fail on collision. No ranges are installed by
+default. ARM64 page size and runtime VA width constrain valid intervals.
+The feature is incompatible with CONFIG_SHARE_POOL. AArch32 processes
+retain their ordinary mapping behavior and cannot use numbered mappings.
